# A SHELL SCRIPT WITH CRLF IS NOT A SHELL SCRIPT.
#
# `bash` reads the carriage return as part of the last word on the line, so `set -euo pipefail`
# becomes `set -euo pipefail\r` and the shell answers `pipefail\r: invalid option name` before a
# single line of the script has run. Four of our own scripts were checked in that way --
# `installer/package-linux.sh` among them, which is the one that builds the Linux release tarball.
# So the tarball could not be built from a clean Linux checkout at all, and the failure does not
# name line endings anywhere in its message.
#
# It happens without anyone choosing it: a file written by a Windows editor gets CRLF, and with no
# rule here git stores exactly what it is handed.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
configure text eol=lf

# ...and the other direction, for the files a Windows shell reads. `cmd.exe` is the one that really
# minds: a `.bat` with bare LF can run the wrong line after a `goto`.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary, so git never looks inside them for line endings to convert.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.vsix binary
*.msi binary
*.polb binary
*.ttf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
